  -- Valoree Swanson / climate-change  (chair 5 -> 5, REPAIR)
    ('7c2c52a2-4047-4090-bc88-e543aaaeba50'::uuid, 'f1e44d66-5d27-4b51-b54f-b7ace86f6a3c'::uuid, '86d893a1-c1a2-4bbf-b4e5-69ec43221194'::uuid, '5f1403f3-90b6-491f-ba54-3c8e46a5ae26'::uuid, 'Citation repaired 2026-10-07 (migration 1903); THE CHAIR IS UNCHANGED. Every source on this row was a page about the person -- a Texas Legislature member page, a Wikipedia or Ballotpedia biography -- and none of them is evidence of a position. They are kept below; the citation now points at what this member actually did. 🔑 THE RECORD: 87(R) SB13, voted to bar state investment in companies that boycott energy firms, the House 105-40, Record 687. ⚠ WHAT THIS DOES AND DOES NOT SETTLE. The instrument places this member firmly on their side of the ladder; it does not by itself separate the rungs WITHIN that side, and the chair keeps the placement the original research gave it. Registered as a ladder-reachability finding rather than silently absorbed. ⚖ CARRY GATE: the Season 2 text of this rung differs from Season 1 and was read in both revisions -- Season 1 rejected climate policy in general; Season 2 names the specific case of ending clean-energy subsidies and mandates, which the general rejection contains', ARRAY['http://journals.house.texas.gov/hjrnl/87r/pdf/87RDAY39FINAL.PDF', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Valoree_Swanson']::text[]),
  -- Vikki Goodwin / climate-change  (chair 2 -> 0, BLANK LADDER_SCOPE)
    ('f39b0865-c923-428a-b940-09138c09e4e8'::uuid, 'f1e44d66-5d27-4b51-b54f-b7ace86f6a3c'::uuid, '86d893a1-c1a2-4bbf-b4e5-69ec43221194'::uuid, '5f1403f3-90b6-491f-ba54-3c8e46a5ae26'::uuid, 'Blanked 2026-10-07 (migration 1903). ⚠ THE SEASON 2 RUNG AT THIS VALUE ASKS A DIFFERENT QUESTION. A repair may not move a chair, and this chair cannot be carried: SPEED BECAME MECHANISM. Season 1 rung 2 graded how fast ("rapidly transition to renewable energy and phase out fossil fuels by 2030"); Season 2 rung 2 grades how ("Fund clean energy with major subsidies, tax credits, and public investment"). Wanting faster decarbonisation does not say which mechanism you prefer. Ruled the same way in Massachusetts (migration 1902). The finding is preserved here, but it cannot seat the Season 2 chair. The sources examined are kept below so the next reader does not re-derive this. This is a blank, not a deletion -- the chair may well be re-researchable, and that is an invitation.', ARRAY['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vikki_Goodwin']::text[]),
  -- Vikki Goodwin / fossil-fuels  (chair 2 -> 2, REPAIR)
